AUDIO: 'retune itself while it\'s running. And this matters because quantum computers are extremely sensitive machines. Tiny changes in temperature or even electronics or hardware can slowly push its calculations out of calibration. And'

[25:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if this happens, then currently engineers might need to stop the computation completely and then adjust thousands of control settings and then start again. And that\'s a major problem if eventually we want to run these'

[25:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quantum computers continuously for days or months. So here\'s Google\'s solution to this. They use reinforcement learning together with quantum error correction. Now this is very technical, but I\'ll try to dumb it down. What quantum error'

[25:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'correction does is it produces a stream of signals showing that errors are happening. These are like warning signs. Well, Google basically feeds these signals into an AI agent. The agent watches how these error patterns change'

[26:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then adjust thousands of control parameters including the frequencies, phases, and strengths of the signals that control the qubits. You can think of it like tuning an orchestra while the musicians continue playing. And then'

[26:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'after running this for a lot of rounds using reinforcement learning, the AI kind of learns which parameters to tweak in order to reduce the errors the most. In fact, they say that this reinforcement learning fine-tuning'

